<h1>One request, three independent gates - and what each one catches if the others fail</h1>

<div class="col root"><div class="card"><div class="t">Client request</div></div>
<div class="arrow">↓</div>
<div class="card amber"><div class="t">LAYER C - Identity Proxy (the bouncer)</div><div class="d">Terminates TLS · verifies the session/JWT · extracts app + owner from the URL · adds X-App-ID · no raw port is ever exposed</div>
<div class="d">What it does NOT catch: anything that happens AFTER a legitimate, authenticated user is already inside their own workspace.</div>
<div class="d">If this were the only layer: a legitimate user runs malicious code inside their own container - the proxy only watches the front door, never sees this &quot;east-west&quot; traffic.</div></div>
<div class="arrow">↓</div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">LAYER B - VLAN / ipvlan (the soundproof wall)</div><div class="d">Every tenant's traffic is 802.1Q-tagged (vlan.id) on a dedicated sub-interface · the kernel makes cross-tenant packets physically unroutable</div>
<div class="d">What it does NOT catch: a kernel escape on the host itself.</div>
<div class="d">If this were the only layer: a zero-day in the real Linux kernel lets an attacker break out of runc entirely - from there, one `ip link` command on the host undoes every VLAN you configured.</div></div>
<div class="arrow">↓</div>
<div class="card green"><div class="t">LAYER A - gVisor (the straitjacket)</div><div class="d">Application syscalls never reach the real host kernel - the Sentry emulates them in a Go userspace process (see the architecture diagram above)</div>
<div class="d">What it does NOT catch: an attacker already inside the sandbox scanning the LOCAL network.</div>
<div class="d">If this were the only layer: gVisor hands normal Ethernet frames to the host bridge - nothing stops the sandboxed app from nmap-scanning a neighboring tenant's container on the same unsegmented bridge.</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">Each layer's blind spot is exactly the next layer's job</div><div class="d">that's why all three run together, not any one alone.</div></div></div>

</body>
